�����z�V9�P�bt1�����+6�V��H���u��M�����/�����a�z�xT���˴�m����|TE�P�����3$�D��g��zr��ۤ���Y�a(��uN�R =/�E+Q��", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-22"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "OpenLB Open Library of Bioscience", "url": "https://ngdc.cncb.ac.cn/openlb/publication/OLB-PM-37602328", "domain": "ngdc.cncb.ac.cn", "snippet": "- Wafik S El-Deiry: Laboratory of Translational Oncology and Experimental Cancer Therapeutics, Warren Alpert Medical School, Brown University, Providence, RI, United States.", "date": "2023-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-01"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Unbiased in vivo preclinical evaluation of anticancer drugs identifies effective therapy for the treatment of pancreatic adenocarcinoma | PNAS", "url": "https://www.pnas.org/doi/abs/10.1073/pnas.1920240117", "domain": "pnas.org", "snippet": "To identify effective PDAC therapies, we leveraged a syngeneic orthotopic PDAC transplant mouse model to perform a large-scale, in vivo screen of 16 single-agent and 41 two-drug targeted therapy combinations in mice.", "date": "2020-12-01", "last_updated": "2025-03-25"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Preclinical Models of Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma and Their Utility in Immunotherapy Studies", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/13/3/440", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "In the KPC GEMM model, the combination of a CD40 agonist and gemcitabine induced tumor regression in 30% of tumor-bearing mice [74,75], similar to the objective response rate in patients [75].", "date": "2021-01-25", "last_updated": "2026-08-13"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Schrödinger, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/ee6d0cc13d500f7740bc81b30e6d5978", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "In KRAS G12C tumor xenograft mouse models, SGR-4174 monotherapy demonstrated dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and a decrease in the tumor PD biomarker pERK.SGR-4174 was examined in combination with KRAS G12C inhibitor sotorasib and MEK inhibitor trametinib in vitro and in vivo.\nWhen combined with sotorasib, SGR-4174 demonstrated deeper and more sustained inhibition of the KRAS signaling pathway, stronger suppression of cell proliferation in 3D cell culture, and tumor regression in xenograft models compared to single agent activity.\nIn addition, when combined with trametinib, SGR-4174 suppressed KRAS pathway reactivation by MEK inhibition and led to robust tumor cell death and tumor stasis.\nCompared to other published SOS1 inhibitors (BI-3406, MRTX0902), SGR-4174 is more potent, and demonstrated similar anti-tumor activity and target engagement at a lower dose both in monotherapy and combination therapy studies in xenograft tumor models.\nSGR-4174 was well tolerated as monotherapy and in combination with sotorasib or trametinib.SGR-4174 demonstrated favorable PK properties across preclinical species and toxicity profile was well characterized.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "In vivo Mouse Models of Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://pancreapedia.org/node/9843", "domain": "pancreapedia.org", "snippet": "The addition of a stearate to gemcitabine (GEM) generated a fatty acid GEM derivative termed stearoyl gemcitabine (4NSG) that demonstrated more potent anticancer effects: greatly reduced cell proliferation, enhanced apoptosis and induced cytotoxicity in 2D and 3D PDAC cell cultures.", "date": "2021-09-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}], "raw": {"id": "852d4217-0d67-4b7a-8f61-e86b752accc0"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:34:23+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q027", "objective": "HQL-79 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["HQL-79"], "mode": "objective", "case_id": "q027-obj", "query": "HQL-79 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HQL-79 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 16423.2, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "An efficient drug delivery system to block pancreatic cancer", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40127723/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Hence, we developed an efficient drug delivery system by encapsulation of Que. into liposomes and conjugation with hyaluronidase (HAase) at liposome surface, termed as HQL.\nIn the presence of HAase, HQL were predominantly accumulated at tumor with enhanced permeability and retention effect.\nTreatment of xenografted tumor mice with HQL gave rise to suppressed tumor growth, while no toxic effects were observed in mice.\nHQL demonstrated the strong ability to inhibit cell proliferation, promote cell apoptosis, and induce arrest at G2/M cell cycle in pancreatic cancer lines, three-dimensional cultured cell spheroids and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC)-derived organoids.\nMechanistically, HQL downregulated expression of cell cycle-associated protein (CCNB1, CDK1 and PLK1) and cell apoptosis-associated factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2.\nIn summary, HQL degraded HA in the tumor microenvironment to enhance nano-particle penetration and inhibited tumor cell growth, eliciting efficacy of anti-tumor therapy.\nThereof, HQL may provide a novel efficient drug delivery approach for the adjuvant treatment of pancreatic cancer.", "date": "2025-06-10", "last_updated": "2025-09-17"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Unbiased in vivo preclinical evaluation of anticancer drugs identifies effective therapy for the treatment of pancreatic adenocarcinoma - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33199632/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "To identify effective PDAC therapies, we leveraged a syngeneic orthotopic PDAC transplant mouse model to perform a large-scale, in vivo screen of 16 single-agent and 41 two-drug targeted therapy combinations in mice.\nAmong 57 drug conditions screened, combined inhibition of heat shock protein (Hsp)-90 and MEK was found to produce robust suppression of tumor growth, leading to an 80% increase in the survival of PDAC-bearing mice with no significant toxicity.\nMechanistically, we observed that single-agent MEK inhibition led to compensatory activation of resistance pathways, including components of the PI3K/AKT/mTOR signaling axis, which was overcome with the addition of HSP90 inhibition.\nThe combination of HSP90(i) + MEK(i) was also active in vitro in established human PDAC cell lines and in vivo in patient-derived organoid PDAC transplant models.\nThese findings encourage the clinical development of HSP90(i) + MEK(i) combination therapy and highlight the power of clinically relevant in vivo model systems for identifying cancer therapies.", "date": "2020-12-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-20"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Preclinical Models of Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma and Their Utility in Immunotherapy Studies", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/13/3/440", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "In the KPC GEMM model, the combination of a CD40 agonist and gemcitabine induced tumor regression in 30% of tumor-bearing mice [74,75], similar to the objective response rate in patients [75].", "date": "2021-01-25", "last_updated": "2026-08-13"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "In vivo Mouse Models of Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://pancreapedia.org/node/9843", "domain": "pancreapedia.org", "snippet": "Finally, using three unique PC PDX mouse lines, a unique PGAM1 inhibitor (KH3) was evaluated in these mice and proven to downregulate glycolysis, mitochondrial respiration and glutathione metabolism leading to cell cycle arrest, increased apoptosis and reduced tumor volume (125).", "date": "2021-09-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Molecular Mechanism of Autophagy and Its Regulation by ...", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7999886/", "domain": "ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Evidence has revealed the efficacy of CQ and HCQ in hindering cancer cell proliferation through autophagy inhibition in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma and bladder cancers [109].\nHQL is not effective as a monotherapy but shows improvement when used in combination with gemcitabine with nab-paclitaxel.\nParallel inhibition of ERK synergistically enhances HQL inhibition.", "date": "2021-03-10", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Radioimmunotherapy of Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7072553/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Many of the in vivo studies demonstrated a significant improvement in survival in the mice who received α or β RIT compared to the control treatments (untreated or unlabelled antibody).\nTypically, survival improved with higher RIT doses, multiple treatment cycles and the use of a combination therapy approach when delivered within tolerable limits.", "date": "2020-02-19", "last_updated": "2025-09-22"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Experimental models of pancreas cancer: what has been the impact ...", "url": "https://www.jci.org/articles/view/191945", "domain": "jci.org", "snippet": "Orthotopic models are derived by implantation of patient- or mouse-derived pancreas cancer cells or intact tumor pieces directly into the mouse pancreas (Figure 2, A–C).", "date": "2025-08-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-22"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma: Current and Evolving Therapies", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/1422-0067/18/7/1338", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "Similarly, targeting of miR-21 using lentiviral vectors stimulated angiogenesis, enhanced gemcitabine delivery and provoked tumour regression [259,260,261].", "date": "2017-06-22", "last_updated": "2026-08-11"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Results", "url": "https://apc.amegroups.org/article/view/5867/html", "domain": "apc.amegroups.org", "snippet": "Ultimately, the concept of therapeutic synergy in multi-agent chemotherapeutic administration has resulted in the routine use of gemcitabine/nab-paclitaxel, or alternatively 5-fluorouracil/irinotecan/oxaliplatin, as standards of clinical care in pancreatic cancer.", "date": "2020-12-31", "last_updated": "2025-12-13"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Current and emerging therapies for patients with advanced ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011058/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Inhibitors of CSF1R have shown success in producing tumour regression and improved survival in mouse models through reprogramming of tumour-associated macrophages to enhance antigen presentation and promote an antitumour T-cell response.^36^ A preliminary study reported durable responses in five of 31 heavily pretreated patients with pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma who received a CSF1R inhibitor in combination with nivolumab.", "date": "2004-09-01", "last_updated": "2025-08-31"}], "raw": {"id": "3beb01a0-4f9c-4d5f-9f73-7aa51d06e77f"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:34:24+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q032", "objective": "PHY-003 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["PHY-003"], "mode": "objective", "case_id": "q032-obj", "query": "PHY-003 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma animal models", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "PHY-003 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma animal models", "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 959.7, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] This electronic thesis or dissertation has been downloaded from the ...", "url": "https://kclpure.kcl.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/336822127/2025_Langdon_Sophie_19071106_ethesis.pdf", "domain": "kclpure.kcl.ac.uk", "snippet": "In vivo, MTCQ1 was shown to grow and metastasize when transplanted into syngeneic mice and exhibit molecular and pathological changes that resemble those that occur in their human OSCC counterparts.\n...\n4.4.1 An 8 Gy dose of radiation slows tumour growth compared to untreated \ntumours in MTCQ1 HNSCC murine mouse model\n...\nGy EBRT were found to slow, and in some cases reduce, tumour growth relative to untreated tumours (Figure 4.3 A) with differences starting from day 19 post-tumour cell injection (5 days post-radiotherapy treatment), where untreated tumour volume averaged 101.8 ± 68.9 mm3 versus a treated tumour volume of 43.3 ± 4.9 mm3.\n...\nThe first therapeutic study carried out in this chapter in the MTCQ1 showed a dose of 8 Gy inhibits tumour growth with\ndifferences in average tumour volume  being seen from day 17 onwards.\n...\nThis work demonstrated the therapeutic efficacy of a single dose of 8 Gy compared to a fractionated schedule of 6 x 2 Gy as well as providing evidence to support the literature stating that tumour size at the start of treatment impacts therapeutic outcomes.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-27"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov › articles › PMC7944998Head and neck squamous cell carcinoma - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7944998/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "For patients who benefit from these agents, responses seem to be durable^7–9^.", "date": "2020-11-26", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Current and Emerging Molecular Therapies for Head and Neck ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8582411/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Currently, only cetuximab, pembrolizumab and nivolumab are approved by the FDA as molecular therapies for HNSCC.", "date": "2021-10-30", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging tyrosine kinase inhibitors for head and neck cancer", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9987561/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Treatment of three HNSCC PDX models with anlotinib reduced tumor cell growth *in vivo* (31).\n...\nTo date, clinical investigations of TKIs in HNSCC have not yielded substantial improvements in patient outcomes.\nThis has been particularly true when TKIs have been evaluated as monotherapy.", "date": "2022-09-21", "last_updated": "2025-10-22"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov › articles › PMC12732836Current and Emerging Protein Biomarkers for the Diagnosis and ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12732836/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In a clinical trial, patients with recurrent or metastatic HNSCC and mutant HRAS, Tipifarnib treatment resulted in an objective response rate of 55% and a median overall survival of 15.4 months [108].", "date": "2025-12-15", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical update on head and neck cancer: molecular biology and ongoing challenges", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6629629/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Promising results from pre-clinical models of HNSCC showed inhibition of proliferation, migration, invasion and EMT^70^.", "date": "2019-07-15", "last_updated": "2025-12-07"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Overview of current and future biologically based targeted therapies ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2660327/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "But, single-agent anti-angiogenic drugs have not shown activity in unselected HNSCC patients, with a response rate of less than 4%.", "date": "2009-03-02", "last_updated": "2025-09-03"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Novel Systemic Treatment Modalities Including Immunotherapy and Molecular Targeted Therapy for Recurrent and Metastatic Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/1422-0067/23/14/7889", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "Similarly, NOTCH1-mutant HNSCC xenografts treated with PI3K pathway inhibitors demonstrated elevated cell death and significant tumor volume reduction in vivo [87].", "date": "2022-07-17", "last_updated": "2026-08-11"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Systemic therapy strategies for head-neck carcinomas", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3544206/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In preclinical studies, gene therapy involving the replacement of mutated p53 led to reduced growth of HNSCC and increased radiochemical sensitivity [118].", "date": "2012-12-20", "last_updated": "2025-12-16"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Phytochemicals as Potential Chemopreventive and ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2021.