Malignancies—The Last ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8750348/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|2|Gilteritinib|XOSPATA Astellas Pharma US, Inc., Northbrook, IL, USA|FDA: 28 November 2018 EMA: 24 October 2019| |FLT3 ^3^, AXL ^4^, ALK ^5^|Oral|Acute Myeloid Leukemia|Myalgia, arthralgia, increased levels of transaminases, fatigue, malaise, fever, diarrhea, dyspnea, edema, rash, pneumonia, sepsis, renal impairment|[69,70]|", "date": "2021-12-24", "last_updated": "2026-09-26"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Recent advances in targeted therapies in acute myeloid ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10039574/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "After years of stagnation in new drug approvals for AML, there has been a rapid expansion of the armamentarium against this disease since 2017.", "date": "2023-03-25", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Immunotherapy for Acute Myeloid Leukemia", "url": "https://d-nb.info/1325200867/34", "domain": "d-nb.info", "snippet": "Approval has also been granted for GO monotherapy for relapsed/refractory (R/R) AML based on the phase 2 Mylofrance-1 study that reported a 26%", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-13"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Acute Myeloid Leukemia | PDF | Hematopoietic Stem Cell ...", "url": "https://www.scribd.com/document/421889287/acute-myeloid-leukemia", "domain": "scribd.com", "snippet": "Lestaurtinib (CEP-701) is an orally bioavailable first- of which have shown clinical benefit in clinical trials", "date": "2026-04-15", "last_updated": "2026-04-15"}], "raw": {"id": "13a67bd9-49e2-4c82-9d3b-a223e8e60024"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:34:35+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q047", "objective": "Dual CDK12/13 Inhibitor first regulatory approval or market launch year for acute myeloid leukemia", "anchors": ["Dual CDK12/13 Inhibitor", "CDK12"], "mode": "objective", "case_id": "q047-obj", "query": "Dual CDK12/13 Inhibitor first regulatory approval or market launch year for acute myeloid leukemia", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Dual CDK12/13 Inhibitor first regulatory approval or market launch year for acute myeloid leukemia", "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1405.3, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "CDK12/13 dual inhibitors are potential therapeutics for Acute Myeloid L…", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10330638/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "2023 May 14;202(1):195–198.\n...\nSince 2017, an increasing number of targeted agents or drug combinations have been approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and added to clinical practice, including the BCL2 inhibitor venetoclax1.\n...\nIn summary, our study has demonstrated the efficacy of CDK12/CDK13 dual inhibitors, THZ531 and SR-4835, against both leukemia cell lines and primary leukemia samples, including those that are resistant to Venetoclax-based therapies.\nThese results provide a compelling rationale for exploring the use of CDK12/CDK13 dual inhibitors as a potential therapy following the acquisition of resistance to Venetoclax-based combination treatment or as a first-line treatment option for leukemia samples with Venetoclax-resistant molecular signatures.", "date": "2023-05-14", "last_updated": "2026-09-20"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "CDK12/13 dual inhibitors are potential therapeutics for acute myeloid leukemia", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/bjh.18843", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "CDK12/13 dual inhibitors are potential therapeutics for acute myeloid leukemia\n...\n05/14/2023", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-11-01"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Design, Synthesis, and Biological Evaluation of Novel ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39948720/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Here, we report the discovery of compound **12b** as a novel, potent, orally available covalent CDK12/13 dual inhibitor with a promising safety profile and robust *in vivo* antitumor properties.", "date": "2025-02-27", "last_updated": "2026-02-09"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "link.springer.com · content · pdfCDK12 and CDK13 in oncology: from RNA regulation to ... -...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s13402-025-01131-z.pdf?error=cookies_not_supported&code=2c0b3951-b81f-46d3-ad8f-71a4c6d62a62", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "Consistently, CDK12/13 knockout or knockdown impairs the growth of diverse myeloid leukemia cell lines.\n...\nPublisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to juris­ dictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.\n...\n85. L. Savoy, N. Long, H. Lee et al., CDK12/13 dual inhibitors are \npotential therapeutics for acute myeloid leukemia.\nBr.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Cyclins and Cyclin‐Dependent Kinases: Structure ...", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/mco2.70898", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "|Romaciclib|CDK8/19|Inhibitor|Acute myeloid leukemia|NCT06191263|2|Completed|", "date": "2026-09-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "CDK12 and CDK13 in oncology: from RNA regulation to therapeutic ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13402-025-01131-z?error=cookies_not_supported&code=6b4ea213-82d8-4f45-929b-d04a472f2841", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "In acute myeloid leukemia (AML), aberrant transcriptional elongation is a hallmark feature [85].\nDual inhibition or genetic loss of CDK12/13 reduces RNA polymerase II elongation rate and processivity by ~50%, with the strongest effects observed in long multi-exonic genes.\n...\nConsistently, *CDK12/13* knockout or knockdown impairs the growth of diverse myeloid leukemia cell lines.", "date": "2026-01-05", "last_updated": "2026-08-25"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Cyclins and Cyclin-Dependent Kinases: Structure, Biological Functions, and Innovative Targeting Strategies in Cancer", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/mco2.70898", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "|Romaciclib|CDK8/19|Inhibitor|Acute myeloid leukemia|NCT06191263|2|Completed|", "date": "2026-09-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "CDK Inhibitors - Selleck Chemicals", "url": "https://www.selleckchem.com/CDK.html?page=inhibitors", "domain": "selleckchem.com", "snippet": "|S8894|SR-4835|SR-4835 is a highly selective dual inhibitor of **CDK12** and **CDK13** with IC50 of 99 nM and Kd of 98 nM for CDK12 and IC50 of 4.9 nM for CDK13.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Cyclin-dependent protein kinases and cell cycle regulation in ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11734941/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "NTogether, none of these inhibitors have obtained FDA approval, and only dinaciclib reached phase III trials. following", "date": "2025-01-13", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 1.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12769584/table/Tab1/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|*Dual target bivalent molecule*|Bifunctional molecule linking CDK12/13 to transcription factor BCL6 at chromatin|CDK-TCIP3|Induces cell death of DLBCL cells (EC~50~ ~ 609 nM after 72 h) with the 10 times greater effect than CDK12/13 inhibitor alone [110]|", "date": "2026-01-01", "last_updated": "2026-02-02"}], "raw": {"id": "6033d088-9b84-4795-b0b0-9f47fea9d914"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:34:36+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q044", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as GPR52 Prostaglandin E2 Receptor EP4, including Small Molecule therapies", "anchors": ["NXE0033744", "NXE744"], "mode": "objective", "case_id": "q044-obj", "query": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as GPR52 Prostaglandin E2 Receptor EP4, including Small Molecule therapies", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as GPR52 Prostaglandin E2 Receptor EP4, including Small Molecule therapies", "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 4117.3, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "www.frontiersin.org › journals › pharmacologyFrontiers | Biologic and small-molecule therapies for ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2026.1948258/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Three selective interleukin (IL)-23p19 antagonists have been approved for both UC and CD; two sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators and a second-generation Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitor have entered routine practice; subcutaneous formulations have altered delivery; and the first formal head-to-head trials, together with placebo-controlled trials incorporating an active reference arm, have begun to replace indirect comparison with direct evidence.\nIn parallel, the ORAL Surveillance trial prompted a class-wide boxed warning that recalibrated, but did not eliminate, the role of JAK inhibitors.\nThree further strategies have generated late-phase data: anti-TL1A antibodies, which target a pathway not previously addressed pharmacologically in IBD; the miR-124 enhancer obefazimod; and the oral IL-23 receptor antagonist peptide icotrokinra.\n...\nThis review synthesises the mechanistic basis, pivotal evidence, comparative effectiveness, safety and regulatory status of approved and investigational therapies for IBD, and how treat-to-target principles, early advanced therapy and emerging biomarkers are reshaping positioning.", "date": "2026-07-25", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Management of inflammatory bowel disease beyond tumor ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9449214/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Novel biologics and small-molecule drugs have been developed for IBD, and the medical treatment options have improved.\nThese drugs include sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators and anti-integrins to block immune cell migration, and cytokine and Janus kinase inhibitors to block immune cell communications.\n...\nBy contrast, SMDs are of low molecular weight (< 1 kDa) and are synthesized chemically.\nSMDs pass easily through cell membranes and have a short half-life.\nTherefore, SMDs can be administered orally and are non-immunogenic [15].\nNovel, approved monoclonal antibodies and SMDs for IBD include anti-integrins, anti-interleukins (ILs), Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors, and sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators [16]; these block immune cell communication or migration.\n...\n|Block immune cell migrations|Anti-adhesion|Natalizumab|mAb|Anti-integrin (α4 subunit)|IV|NA|Approved|NA|NA|Limited data^c^|\n...\n|Block immune cell communications|Anti-cytokine|Ustekinumab|mAb|Anti-IL-12/IL-23 (p40)|IV/SC|Approved|Approved|Approved^a^|Approved|Limited data^c^|\n|Block immune cell communications|Blockage of downstream signaling|Tofacitinib|SMD|Pan-JAK inhibitor|Oral|Approved|NA|Approved|NA|Very limited data^d^|\n...\nImmune cell migration into the gut mucosa can be suppressed by inhibitors of lymphocyte trafficking from regional lymph nodes to the bloodstream and of adhesion to the gut mucosa.\n...\nIntegrin inhibitors prevent lymphocyte infiltration of the gut mucosa, known as gut homing.\n...\nOzanimod is an oral SMD and a selective modulator of S1PR~1~ and S1PR~5~, mainly S1PR~1~ [23].\n...\nTofacitinib is an oral SMD that inhibits JAK1 and JAK3, blocking the downstream JAK/STAT signaling pathway and thus modulating DNA transcription [66].\n...\nUpadacitinib is an oral SMD and a selective JAK1 inhibitor.\n...\nSelective blockers of the IL-23 p19 subunit, such as brazikumab, risankizumab, and mirikizumab, have shown efficacy against CD.", "date": "2022-08-10", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacological Therapy in Inflammatory Bowel Diseases", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10537095/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Despite the different mechanisms of action, there are similarities between the general rates of effectiveness.\nThese similar results were also evidenced in JAK inhibitors and S1p modulators, the last therapeutic classes approved for the treatment of IBD.\n...\nIn the 2010s, there was the development of new synthetic technologies, called “small molecules”, where efficacy was observed in Janus Kinase inhibitors (anti-JAK) and sphingosine-1-phosphate modulators (S1p modulators) [4,6,9].\n...\nThree classes of biological therapies are currently used in the treatment of IBD: (I) anti-TNF, (II) anti-integrins, and (III) anti-interleukins [6,35].\n...\nTofacitinib was the first small molecule released for the treatment of UC, it is a molecule that acts on all JAK, mainly on JAK 1 and 3.\n...\nS1p modulators act as functional antagonists in lymphocytic receptors, inhibiting lymphocytes dependent on this receptor from leaving secondary lymph nodes for peripheral blood, reducing the circulating number of these cells.\n...\nFrom this point of view, a recent review of biologics and small molecules used in IBD points to JAK inhibitors and S1p modulators as drugs with a greater safety profile than TNF antagonists, especially considering drugs with greater selectivity [118].\n...\nAmong small molecules, new therapies with greater selectivity to JAK 1 such as Izencitinib and Peficitinib are under study, in addition to JAK/TYK2 combinations such as Brepocitinib [119].\n...\nAlthough biological therapies are proven to be safe and effective, the development of small molecules assumes the combination of similar effectiveness with the therapeutic convenience of oral administration.", "date": "2023-09-08", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov › articles › PMC6537282New biologics and small molecules in inflammatory bowel ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6537282/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Currently, there are three classes of biologicals available to treat IBD.\nThese are\nantagonists to tumor necrosis factor (TNF), anti-integrins and inhibitors of\ninterleukin (IL) 12/IL-23.\n...\n|JAK inhibitor|Filgotinib|Oral|Small molecule that blocks JAK1|Phase II|–|\n| |Peficitinib|Oral|Small molecule that blocks JAK1, JAK2 and JAK3|–|Phase II|\n| |Tofacitinib^ a ^|Oral|Small molecule that preferentially blocks JAK1 and JAK3|Phase II^ b ^|Phase III|\n| |Upadacitinib|Oral|Small molecule that blocks JAK1|Phase II|Phase II|\n...\n| |AJM-300|Oral|Small molecule that blocks the α4 integrin subunit|–|Phase II|\n...\n| |PTG-100|Oral|Small molecule that targets the α4β7 integrin|–|Phase II|\n...\ncytokines.\n...\nThe small molecule tofacitinib is an oral pan-JAK inhibitor, with preferential\ninhibition of JAK1 and JAK3.\n...\nInitially developed for relapsing–remitting multiple sclerosis, laquinimod is an\noral small molecule with immunomodulatory effects.^\n...\n30 ^ Natalizumab, an anti-α4 integrin antibody, and vedolizumab, an anti-α4ß7 integrin antibody, are efficacious in the treatment of IBD.\n...\n57 ^ Apremilast is an oral small molecule that inhibits PDE4 and is already approved for the treatment of psoriatic arthritis and moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis.", "date": "2019-05-26", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Medications for Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/inflammatory-bowel-disease-ibd/medications-for-inflammatory-bowel-disease", "domain": "merckmanuals.com", "snippet": "|**Glucocorticoids**|Regulation of gene expression|Hydrocortisone (IV) Methylprednisolone (IV) Prednisone (oral) Prednisolone (oral) Budesonide (oral) Budesonide MMX (enteric coated slow release) Hydrocortisone (enema or foam)|\n|** Immunomodulators (antimetabolites)**|Thiopurines|Azathioprine 6-Mercaptopurine (6MP)|\n...\n|**Small Molecules**|Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors|Tofacitinib Upadacitinib|\n| |Sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators|Ozanimod Etrasimod|\n...\n5-ASA (**mesalamine**) is primarily used as a first-line medication for induction and maintenance of remission in mild to moderate ulcerative colitis (1).\n5-ASA blocks production of prostaglandins and leukotrienes and has other beneficial effects on the inflammatory cascade.\n...\nSmall molecules are sometimes used in the management of moderate to severe IBD.\nThey are given orally and lack the immunogenicity associated with monoclonal antibodies.\n**Tofacitinib** is a small-molecule agent that inhibits Janus kinase 1–3 and is used for adult patients with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis (1).\n...\n**Upadacitinib** is a small-molecule agent that selectively inhibits Janus kinase 1 used for patients with moderate to severe Crohn disease or ulcerative colitis (1, 2).\n...\n**Ozanimod** is a sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulator and is used for adults with moderate to severe active ulcerative colitis (1).\n...\n**Etrasimod** is a selective sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulator used for the treatment of moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (1).", "date": "2026-03-06", "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "More recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26).\n...\nThis pipeline encompasses novel mechanisms of action targeting diverse inflammatory pathways, including advanced TNF-α inhibition strategies, next-generation JAK and Tyrosine Kinase 2 (TYK2) inhibitors, cell trafficking modulators, IL-23 pathway antagonists, and innovative approaches targeting costimulation, checkpoint pathways, and tissue repair mechanisms.\n...\nIvarmacitinib (SHR0302) is a selective JAK1 inhibitor designed to block the JAK-signal transducer and activator of transcription pathway, thereby controlling inflammation in UC.\n...\nS1P receptor modulators act as functional antagonists despite being receptor agonists.\nUpon binding to S1PR1 on lymphocyte surfaces, these agents cause persistent receptor internalization and proteasome-mediated degradation, resulting in functional downregulation (43).\n...\nObefazimod (ABX464) is an oral small-molecule drug that selectively upregulates miR-124 expression to reduce inflammation, developed for moderate to severe UC through a novel anti-inflammatory mechanism targeting pro-inflammatory cytokines like IL-17 and IL-6.\n...\nTL1A pathway inhibitors represent the most compelling novel target, with phase 2 data showing clinical remission rates exceeding 25% versus less than 2% for placebo across both treatment-naïve and treatment-experienced populations.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Target-Based Small Molecule Drug Discovery Towards Novel Therapeutics for Inflammatory Bowel Diseases", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ibdjournal/article/27/Supplement_2/S38/6428477?searchresult=1", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "In this comprehensive review, we summarize the recent advances in drug discovery of new small molecules in preclinical or clinical development for treating IBD that target biologically relevant pathways in mucosal inflammation.\nThese include intracellular enzymes (Janus kinases, receptor interacting protein, phosphodiesterase 4, IκB kinase), integrins, G protein-coupled receptors (S1P, CCR9, CXCR4, CB2) and inflammasome mediators (NLRP3), etc.\n...\nIn this review, we aim to summarize the recent advances in drug discovery of new small molecules that regulate biologically relevant signaling pathways such as various intracellular enzymes, G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs), integrins, inflammasomes, and epigenetic regulators under either preclinical or clinical development for the treatment of IBD.\n...\nRecently, Bristol Myers Squibb advanced the first selective and allosteric inhibitor of TYK2, BMS986165 (Figure 11, diagram 7), into phase 2 clinical trial for treating moderate to severe CD (NCT03599622).\n...\nTo date, one S1P receptor modulator, known as fingolimod (Figure 14, diagram 25), has been approved by FDA for the treatment of relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis.^106^ Several S1P receptor modulators are in various stages of clinical studies, and 4 of them are being evaluated in IBD, shown in Figure 14, diagram 25.\n...\nEtrasimod (Figure 14, diagram 32) is in clinical development for treatment of IBD and selectively targets S1P~1~ (EC~50~ = 6.1 nM), S1P~4~ (147 nM), and S1P~5~ (EC~50~ = 24.4 nM) with high selectivity over S1P~2~ and S1P~3~ (EC~50~ > 10 000 nM) in in vitro signaling assays.\n...\nApremilast (Figure 17, diagram 38) is an orally available and PDE4-selective inhibitor that blocks the hydrolysis of cAMP by binding to the catalytic site of the PDE4 enzyme (IC~50 = ~74 nM; Figure 17).\n...\nSignificant progress has been made in the drug discovery and development of new small-molecule candidates that target biologically relevant pathways in the pathogenesis of IBD.\nThe relevant drug targets include JAK, RIP1, S1P receptor, integrin, CCR9, CXCR4, PDE4, LANCL2, NLRP3, IKK2, CB2, P2X7 receptor, and emerging epigenetic target BRD4, which are associated with activation, communication, and trafficking of immune cells.", "date": "2021-11-15", "last_updated": "2026-07-03"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Small Molecule Drugs in Inflammatory Bowel Diseases - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8308576/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "To address these issues, orally administered Small Molecules Drugs (SMDs) that use a broad range of novel pharmacological pathways, such as JAK inhibitors, sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators, and phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitors, have been developed for CD and UC.\n...\nIn IBD, a step-up strategy is currently the standard of care: guidelines mostly advise the use of biomolecular drugs in moderate to severe IBD when first-step medications such as steroids, aminosalicylates, and immunomodulators have failed or are insufficient to induce or maintain remission.\n...\nSmall molecules drugs (SMDs) that use a broad range of novel pharmacological pathways, e.g., Janus kinases (JAKs) inhibitors, sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators, and phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitors, have been developed for IBDs (Figure 1).\n...\nJAKs have therefore become a therapeutic target in IBDs.\n...\nSeveral JAK inhibitors with different selectivity and safety profiles are currently in use, all orally administered (Table 1).\n...\nTofacitinib is a pankinase inhibitor, preferentially inhibiting JAK1 and JAK3 [52].\n...\nUpadacitinib (RINVOQ™, Abbvie, IL, USA) is a JAK1-selective inhibitor, recently approved for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis, with a four-hour half-life [72].\n...\nFilgotinib (JYSELECA™, Gilead, Foster City, CA, USA) is also a JAK1-selective inhibitor, approved for use in rheumatoid polyarthritis.\nIt has a six-hour half-life and is administered once daily [79].\n...\nEtrasimod is an orally administered S1P modulator selectively targeting S1P receptor subtypes 1, 4, and 5 [95].\nEtrasimod was investigated in the phase II OASIS randomized, parallel-group induction trial, which included 156 patients with moderate to severe UC [42] (Table 1).\n...\nApremilast (Otezla, Celgene^®^, Summit, NJ, USA), a PDE4 inhibitor approved for use in psoriatic arthritis and Behçet’s disease, has been evaluated in a phase II, double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT in UC [46].", "date": "2021-06-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Understanding Therapeutic Effects of ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12649937/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Current advanced therapies—including tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-α antagonists, adhesion and trafficking inhibitors (such as anti-integrin agents and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators), interleukin inhibitors, and Janus kinase inhibitors—have improved patient outcomes, but targeting a single inflammatory pathway is often insufficient for long-term disease control.\n...\nOver the past decades, treatment options for IBD have expanded considerably.\nFive major classes of advanced therapies, comprising biologics and oral small molecules, are currently approved, each targeting different points of the inflammatory cascade: tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-α antagonists, adhesion and trafficking inhibitors (including anti-integrin agents and sphingosine1-phosphate receptor modulators), interleukin Inhibitors, and Janus kinase inhibitors.\n...\nBoth anti-integrin agents and S1PR modulators are currently available therapeutic strategies aimed at inhibiting immune cell trafficking in the treatment of IBD.\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that selectively targets the α4β7 integrin on gut-homing lymphocytes, thereby reducing intestinal inflammation without inducing significant systemic immunosuppression.\n...\nThe S1PR modulators are oral small molecules that inhibit immune cell trafficking and are currently approved for the treatment of moderate-to-severe UC.\nThe two approved agents are ozanimod and the more recently authorized etrasimod [72].\nOzanimod selectively targets S1P1 and S1P5 receptors.\n...\nEtrasimod is a second-generation S1PR modulator with high selectivity for S1P1, S1P4 and S1P5, minimal activity on S1PR3, and none on S1PR2.\n...\nThe first JAKi developed for the treatment of IBD was tofacitinib, a first-generation pan-JAK inhibitor.\nIt is approved for the treatment of moderate-to-severe UC based on phase 3 induction and maintenance trials.\n...\nThe two JAK 1 selective inhibitors approved for IBD are upadacitinib and filgotinib.", "date": "2025-10-30", "last_updated": "2026-08-20"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "From Mechanism to Clinic | Inflammatory Bowel Diseases", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ibdjournal/article/31/10/2907/8173239", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Inflammatory bowel disease pathogenesis involves prostaglandin duality.\nEmerging evidence reveals their dual pro/anti-inflammatory roles through receptor-specific signaling.\nTargeting prostaglandin pathways offers novel therapeutic strategies to restore mucosal homeostasis and improve IBD clinical management.\n...\nStandard clinical treatment usually entails the administration of aminosalicylates, corticosteroids, immunosuppressants, and biologics to reduce inflammation, manage symptoms of the disease, and improve the quality of life for patients.\n...\nThrough EP1/EP4 receptors, PGE~2~ activates PLC-IP~3~-Ca²⁺/cAMP-PKA pathways, increasing vascular permeability and visceral pain sensitivity.\n...\nPGE~2~ inhibits epithelial necroptosis through EP4 on IECs and induces the remission of colitis.\n...\nEP4 agonists have shown remarkable performance in the prevention and remission of colitis.\nPrevious studies comparing salazosulfapyridine with novel EP4 agonists revealed that EP4 agonists are more effective in maintaining remission, suggesting their potential as viable alternatives to salazosulfapyridine.^87^ Nevertheless, the PGE~2~-EP4 signaling pathway plays a multifaceted role in immune-related inflammation.", "date": "2025-10-01", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}], "raw": {"id": "3d463ab9-9c01-4d0e-9d4f-8ce52d637042"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:34:37+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q045", "objective": "BMF-500 first regulatory approval or market launch year in acute myeloid leukemia", "anchors": ["BMF-500"], "mode": "objective", "case_id": "q045-obj", "query": "BMF-500 first regulatory approval or market launch year in acute myeloid leukemia", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "BMF-500 first regulatory approval or market launch year in acute myeloid leukemia", "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 4649.2, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "BMF-500(BMF-500) - 药物靶点：FLT3_在研适应症：急性髓性白血病 ...", "url": "https://synapse.zhihuiya.com/drug/0e8fac2d50284b9fac3c91177d16d79c", "domain": "synapse.zhihuiya.com", "snippet": "|首次获批日期-|\n...\nA Phase 1 first-in-human dose-escalation and dose-expansion study of BMF-500, an oral FLT3 inhibitor, in adult patients with acute leukemia.\n|开始日期2023-07-26|\n...\n其共价FLT3抑制剂BMF-500治疗复发/难治性急性白血病的初步临床数据。BMF-500是一种新型、口服可利用的、高效、具选择性的FLT3共价小分子抑制剂。此前的研究表明，BMF-500潜在的脱靶风险较小，对活化的FLT3突变（包括FLT3-ITD和各种酪氨酸激酶结构域突变）具有皮摩尔级的亲和力。在携带FLT3-ITD突变的急性髓系白血病（AML）小鼠模型中，BMF-500使小鼠的肿瘤完全消退，且不需要持续暴露即可维持疗效。BMF-500在治疗FLT3突变的复发/难治性急性白血病患者中表现出令人鼓舞的初步疗效。在可评估的11例FLT3突变患者中，9人出现骨髓原始细胞下降，5人降幅超过50%。同时，在FLT3野生型患者中也观察到疾病控制效果。所有接受治疗的FLT3突变患者（n=18）的中位总生存期（mOS）为3.8个月（无CYP3A4抑制剂）和3.5个月（有CYP3A4抑制剂）。该数据优于历史对照数据，接受过gilteritinib和venetoclax治疗失败后的复发/难治性FLT3突变AML患者的历史mOS仅为2.1个月。BMF-500在各个剂量水平上总体耐受良好，未报告剂量限制性毒性、QT间期延长或因治\n...\n|急性髓性白血病|临床1\n期|美国|Biomea Fusion, Inc.|2023-07-26|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Management of Acute Myeloid Leukemia: A Review - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12939490/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Currently Approved Treatments: In 2017, midostaurin was the first *FLT3* inhibitor agent FDA approved for use in ITD- or TKD-mutated AML.\n...\nThe most recent FDA-approved type II inhibitor, as of 2023, is quizartinib.\n...\n- 32.Law B., Rughwani T., Archer T.C., Kumar L., Lu D., Somanath P., Ma Y., Wang X., Sperandio D., Morris S., et al.\nBmf-500: An Orally Bioavailable Covalent Inhibitor of FLT3 with High Selectivity and Potent Antileukemic Activity in FLT3-Mutated AML.\nBlood.\n2022;140:6191–6192.\ndoi: 10.1182/blood-2022-170445.\n[DOI] [Google Scholar]", "date": "2026-02-18", "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "BMF-500 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse-patsnap-com.libproxy1.nus.edu.sg/drug/0e8fac2d50284b9fac3c91177d16d79c", "domain": "synapse-patsnap-com.libproxy1.nus.edu.sg", "snippet": "|Active Indication Acute Myeloid Leukemia|\n...\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 1|\n|First Approval Date-|\n...\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Acute Myeloid Leukemia|Phase 1|United States|Biomea Fusion, Inc.|26 Jul 2023|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Biomea Fusion | Oncology | Drug Developments", "url": "https://www.pharmacompass.com/pipeline-prospector-drugs-in-development/biomea-fusion/oncology", "domain": "pharmacompass.com", "snippet": "Biomea Fusion Shares Preliminary Data for FLT3 Inhibitor BMF-500 in Acute Leukemia **\n...\nBMF-500, is an investigational covalent FLT3 inhibitor, small molecule drug candidate, which is being evaluated for the treatment of FLT3-gene mutated acute myeloid leukemia.\n...\n**December 09, 2024**\n**\nBiomea Fusion Announces First Patient Dosed with Covalent FLT3 Inhibitor BMF-500 in Relapsed or Refractory Acu...\n**\n...\nBMF-500 is an investigational, novel, orally bioavailable, highly potent and selective covalent small molecule inhibitor of FLT3, which is being investigated in relapsed or refractory acute leukemia.\n...\n**October 17, 2023**\n**\nA Phase 1, Study of BMF-500 in Adults With Acute Leukemia **\n...\n**June 26, 2023**\n**Lead Product(s) : **\nBMF-500\n...\n**Study Phase : **\nIND Enabling\n...\nBiomea Fusion Announces FDA Clearance of Investigational New Drug (IND) Application for Covalent FLT3 Inhibito...\n**\n...\nBMF-500 is an investigational, novel, orally bioavailable, highly potent and selective covalent small molecule inhibitor of FLT3, which is being investigated in relapsed or refractory acute leukemia.\n...\n**May 01, 2023**\n...\nIND Enabling", "date": "2024-12-09", "last_updated": "2025-12-05"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Biomea Fusion, Inc.", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/26bae87fc714320a3f21ce8509ae5e58", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Inactive Indication Acute Myeloid Leukemia [+12]|\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 2|\n|First Approval Ctry.\n/ Loc.-|\n|First Approval Date-|\nBMF-500\n|Target FLT3|\n|--|\n|Mechanism FLT3 inhibitors|\n|Active Org.\nBiomea Fusion, Inc.|\n|Originator Org.\nBiomea Fusion, Inc.|\n|Active Indication Acute Myeloid Leukemia|\n|Inactive Indication-|\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 1|\n|First Approval Ctry.\n/ Loc.-|\n...\n|BMF-500 ( FLT3 )|Acute Myeloid Leukemia More|Phase 1|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "6b6182a8-cf1d-41de-8ea5-3be7003552a3", "url": "https://investors.biomeafusion.com/static-files/6b6182a8-cf1d-41de-8ea5-3be7003552a3", "domain": "investors.biomeafusion.com", "snippet": "In May 2022, we announced the nomination of our second development candidate, BMF-500, a covalent inhibitor of FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3).\nWe announced the U.S. Food and Drug\nAdministration (FDA) clearance of Investigational New Drug application (IND) for BMF-500 in relapsed or refractory (R/R) acute leukemias (AML) in May 2023, and we dosed our first patient in October 2023.\nBMF-500 is being evaluated in R/R\n...\nIn May 2022, we announced the nomination of our second development candidate, BMF-500, a third-generation covalent inhibitor of activating mutations of the FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3), which are the most frequent genetic alteration in AML and are associated with poor prognosis.\n...\nIn May 2023, we announced FDA clearance of our IND to study BMF-\n500 in a Phase 1 study (COVALENT-103) examining safety and efficacy in patients with relapsed or refractory acute\nleukemia with FLT3 wild-type and FLT3 mutations, including those with MLLr/NPM1 mutations.\nIn October 2023, we announced the first patient has been dosed with BMF-500 in relapsed or refractory acute leukemia.\nDose escalation completion and selection of the recommended Phase 2 dose are expected in 2024.\n...\nIn May 2022, we announced our second development candidate, BMF-500, a covalent inhibitor of FLT3.\nBMF-500 is being developed as a highly potent and selective, covalent, small molecule inhibitor of FLT3, that is designed to bind irreversibly to a reactive cysteine in the kinase active site.\n...\nIn addition, our lead product candidate, BMF-219, is in clinical development, and we dosed the first patient with our second product candidate, BMF-500, in October 2023, following clearance of our IND by the FDA in May 2023.\n...\nWe announced the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) clearance of Investigational New Drug application (IND) for \nBMF-500 in relapsed or refractory acute leukemias in May 2023, and we dosed our first patient in October 2023.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-02-11"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Acute Myeloid Leukemia with FLT3/ITD Mutation - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/disease/886f7953ced644f099c38c9af9cd5d80", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Biomea Fusion Announces Preliminary Data from Ongoing COVALENT-103 Study of Investigational Covalent FLT3 Inhibitor BMF-500 in Relapsed or Refractory Acute Leukemia\n...\nBiomea Fusion to Host Conference Call to Present initial Clinical Data from Phase I COVALENT-103 Study of BMF-500, a Covalent FLT3 Inhibitor, in Relapsed or Refractory Acute Leukemia\nPhase 1Clinical ResultAACRASH", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-30"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Form 8-K for Biomea Fusion INC filed 05/02/2023", "url": "https://investors.biomeafusion.com/static-files/e8d3da6b-2d45-436b-a8eb-b72bfb5092bc", "domain": "investors.biomeafusion.com", "snippet": "Date of Report (Date of earliest event reported): May 1, 2023\n...\nOn May 1, 2023, Biomea Fusion, Inc.\n(the “Company”) issued a press release titled, “Biomea Fusion Announces FDA Clearance of Investigational New\nDrug (IND) Application for Covalent FLT3 Inhibitor BMF-500 in Relapsed or Refractory Acute Leukemia.”\n...\nBMF-500, a novel 3rd\ngeneration covalent inhibitor of fms-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3), is the second investigational compound, discovered and developed by Biomea’s FUSION™ System, to advance to the clinic.\n...\nPhase I study (COVALENT-103) of\nBMF-500 will examine its safety and efficacy in patients with relapsed or refractory acute leukemia with FLT3 wild-type and FLT3 mutations, including those with MLLr / NPM1 mutations.\n...\n(“Biomea” or “the company”) (Nasdaq: BMEA), a clinical- stage biopharmaceutical company dedicated to discovering and developing novel covalent small molecules to treat and improve the lives of patients\nwith genetically defined cancers and metabolic diseases, today announced that the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has cleared the company’s\nInvestigational New Drug (IND) application to begin a Phase I\ntrial (COVALENT-103) of BMF-500, an investigational covalent FLT3 inhibitor, in adult\npatients with relapsed or refractory acute leukemia.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Biomea Fusion (BMEA) FDA Approvals", "url": "https://www.marketbeat.com/stocks/NASDAQ/BMEA/fda-events/", "domain": "marketbeat.com", "snippet": "### BMF-500 FDA Regulatory Timeline and Events\nBMF-500 is a drug developed by Biomea Fusion for the following indication: Orally Bioavailable Covalent Inhibitor of FLT3.\nThis drug is under review by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA).\n...\nBiomea Fusion, Inc.\nannounced updated preliminary clinical data from the ongoing Phase I COVALENT-103 trial of BMF-500 in adults with relapsed or refractory (\"R/R\") acute leukemia (\"AL\").\n...\nIn the past two years, Biomea Fusion (BMEA) has not received FDA approval for any therapies.\nHowever, the company does have drugs under review or in active clinical development.\n...\nIn the past two years, Biomea Fusion (BMEA) has reported FDA regulatory activity for the following drugs: icovamenib, BMF-650, BMF-500, COVALENT-111 and BMF-219.", "date": "2026-08-24", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "在研适应症：FLT3阳性急性髓性白血病 - 新药情报", "url": "https://synapse.zhihuiya.com/drug/98b49877cc7e4bb2886113b0d4d98908", "domain": "synapse.zhihuiya.com", "snippet": "在研适应症\nFLT3阳性急性髓性白血病\n...\n临床前\n首次获批日期\n...\nDaiichi will make and supply the med, while European pharma Genesis will head up medical affairs, market access and commercialization efforts in these countries under undisclosed financial terms, the companies said in a release.Vanflyta has been approved in Europe since 2023 as a treatment option for adults with newly diagnosed FLT3\n-ITD-positive acute myeloid leukemia (AML) in combination with standard induction and consolidation therapies, plus as a solo maintenance therapy following consolidation chemo.Approximately 18,000 people are diagnosed with AML annually in Europe, according to Daiichi, with cancer-driving FLT3-ITD mutations occurring in about 25% to 30% of all patients with the disease.“\nBy combining our scientific expertise with the strong regional footprint of Genesis Pharma, we aim to accelerate access to Vanflyta for patients with newly-diagnosed FLT3-ITD positive AML and ultimately help improve outcomes in this high-risk population,” Markus Kosch, M.D., head of Daiichi’s Europe and Canada oncology business division, said in the release.Vanflyta was first approved in Japan four years before U.S. and European regulators signed off on the drug.\n...\nWhen Vanflyta eventually hit the market in the U.S., albeit with a boxed warning for QT prolongation and other heart risks, it became the first FLT3 inhibitor approved across three phases (induction, consolidation and maintenance) of treating the common blood cancer.Still, the company had its work cut out for it, as Novartis’ Rydapt and Astellas’ Xospata were years ahead of Vanflyta with their respective U.S. approvals for FLT3-mutated AML.\n...\nAbout COVALENT-103 COVALENT-103 is a multicenter, open-label, non-randomized trial seeking to evaluate the safety and efficacy of BMF-500, a twice daily oral treatment, in adult patients with relapsed or refractory acute leukemia with FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3) wild-type and FLT3 mutations.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-30"}], "raw": {"id": "4e43c59a-99f8-4852-93e3-47fdddc5828d"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:34:38+00:00"}
